[PARSER]

The parser consists of a tokenizer that converts the original string to tokens
and an expander that expands environmental veriables.

[TOKENIZER]

Potential for binary tree creation. Also could be done later on based on the
created tokens. Main goal should be proper token creation.

Creates seperate tokens out of a string.

We want tokens to be created with context for live syntac checking but the
tokens and their context should be in seperate arrays at the end.

example:

	This is what a token during creation should look like. It can easily ba
	added to a t_list that can later on be transformed into two seperate arrays.

typedef struct s_token
{
	char	*str;			//	The tokenized string
	int		*type;			//	The context (or type) of the token
}	t_token;

After the creation and live syntax checking we want our list of tokens to be
transformed into two arrays. One for the strings (so we can use that array as
input for execve and so on (general uniformity).

example:

	This is what the final struct can look like that get's created out of the
	list of tokens at the end of the tokenization process.

typedef struct s_lineinfo
{
	char	*str;
	char	**tokenstr;
	int		*tokentype;
}


[EXPANDER]

The expander takes a string and replaces all veriables in it with their values
found in a given environment.

The expander should run on the already finished tokens.

Certain tokens can't be expanded: f.e. here document doesn't expand '$'.

Since double qoutes can be expanded we need to look out for delimiters that set
the end of a variable:

	[VARIABLE DELIMITERS]

		' '		(space)
		'<'		(redirection in)
		'>'		(redirection out)
		'|'		(pipe)
		'$'		(dollar)
		'='		(equal)
		'+'		(plus)
		'-'		(minus)
		'&'		(ampersand)
		'''		(qoute)
		'\n'	(newline)

Recursive approach: Encounter '$' -> call func to determine full length of the
variable. Look up the value for the variable in env and return a pointer to the
it's value from env (Skip the pre that set's the variable f.e. "PATH=").
